  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIn WebKit it seems `<meta http-equiv=refresh content=0; URL=#x>` doesn't go through fragment navigation but instead does a regular navigation to that URL (loading the page again).\n\nIn Chromium and Gecko, and per spec AFAICT, this results in a fragment navigation, which can cause various events to be fired, e.g. `beforematch`, and could therefore be [used to cause XSS](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2006870) if a meta refresh can be injected. (The spec disallows navigating to `javascript:` URLs from meta refresh.)\n\nI think WebKit's approach is reasonable, and maybe more closely matches developer expectations since \"refresh\" could be understood to be closer to \"reload\" than \"scroll to this element\".\n\nMaybe needs a new parameter to \"navigate\" to check in step 14.",
